<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:16:Real Mode">
<!--
    kuna #1288: in an x86 real-mode image, every near CALL/JMP/Jcc rel16 above
    linear 0x10000 branched into the first 64 KiB.

    Real-mode addresses are linear, CS*16 + IP, and a code segment need not
    start on a 64 KiB boundary. kuna's GH-7418 fix (tests/stages/
    gh7418-x86-call-rel16-mask.xml) masks a rel16 target to its low 16 bits,
    which is right for a 0x66-prefixed branch in 32-bit code but was also
    applied to 16-bit code: a DOS program loaded at segment 0x1000 lost every
    call target, so discovery found 12 of its functions instead of 330, and
    calls landed in data as phantom functions.

    The bytes sit where that program's main code segment does (CS 0x2b58,
    linear 0x2b580), which spans the 64 KiB line at 0x30000:

        2b680  e8 8d 49     CALL 0x30010      ; CS:0100, 0x0103 + 0x498d = IP 0x4a90
        2b683  c3           RET
        30010  b8 07 00     MOV  AX,0x7
        30013  c3           RET

    Stage: P1/P2 decode (x86 SLEIGH spec fix, no option). `rel16` now splits
    on the decode mode: with a 0x66 prefix (32-bit code) the GH-7418 mask
    stays; in real mode the target is the linear sum; in 16-bit protected
    mode the offset wraps inside its selector, as upstream computes it (see
    kuna-x86-16-protected-rel16.xml). The decoder does not know CS, so a
    branch that wraps IP past 0xffff is not modeled.

    Assert 1 is the decoded target, assert 2 forbids the masked 0x10,
    assert 3 forbids 0x20010 (upstream's wrap inside the 64 KiB block of the
    call, which assumes CS is a multiple of 0x1000), and assert 4 is the call
    reaching the function that is there.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x2b680">
e88d49c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x30010">
b80700c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x2b680" name="f"/>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x30010" name="g"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>disassemble 0x2b680 0x2b684</com>
  <com>lo fu f</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #1: CALL rel16 at 0x2b680 (CS 0x2b58) reaches the linear target 0x30010 across the 64 KiB line" min="1" max="1">^0x0002b680: CALL +0x30010$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #2: the target is not masked into the first 64 KiB (0x10)" min="0" max="0">CALL +0x10$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #3: the target is not wrapped into the call's own 64 KiB block (0x20010)" min="0" max="0">0x20010</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="real-rel16 #4: f calls g" min="1" max="1">^  g\(\);$</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
